# LINE ENDINGS: LF IN THE INDEX AND IN EVERY CHECKOUT (ROADMAP section 58).
# The index has always held LF for every text file. What varied was the WORKING TREE: Git for Windows
# sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, so a checkout manufactured CRLF for any file no rule below pinned
# (measured 2026-09-30: 421 files CRLF, 260 LF, 4 mixed). Every tool then wrote whichever ending it
# preferred, and this file grew one pin per incident - the *.sh hooks, the cheatsheet trio, the knowledge
# store, the review-relay fixtures - each after a fresh Windows clone broke a gate. The default below ends
# that class: text is LF everywhere, whatever core.autocrlf says. `text=auto` still lets git detect a
# binary file by its content, so an unlisted binary is never converted.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- EXCEPTIONS. Each one is a file whose bytes must NOT be LF text. -----------------------------------

# Byte-determinism for the hash-pinned registrar: identical EOL in every checkout so the build-time SHA-256
# pin (register-plugin-hash.iss) matches the packed bytes the uninstaller verifies (GetSHA256OfFile). See
# docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-19-installer-registration-convergence.md Task 5.
installer/_shared/register-plugin.ps1 text eol=crlf

# cmd.exe batch files: CRLF, which is what cmd.exe is written for.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Golden wire-capture oracles must never be line-ending normalized (they contain raw protobuf bytes,
# incl. 0x0a). `text=auto` would already detect most of them by content; this makes it unconditional.
*.bin binary

# The bundled AiSaveDev extension ships to a browser byte for byte (install-extension.ps1 copies it):
# no line-ending conversion, so every checkout carries exactly the committed bytes.
review-relay/extension/aisavedev/** -text
